[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The failure is in the routing control-plane, not the physical link. Specifically, the FRRouting BGP daemon on router1 is down. Evidence: pings from pc1 to pc2 return “Destination Net Unreachable” sourced by 195.11.14.1 (router1), and from pc2 to pc1 by 200.1.1.1 (router2), showing both routers only know their directly connected LANs. On router1, frr.service reports “Active: failed (failed)” and “bgpd is not running,” so no BGP session forms. Router2’s FRR is running, and “show ip bgp summary” shows neighbor 193.10.11.1 stuck in Active, consistent with the peer being down.

The inter-AS physical link is healthy: ethtool on router1:eth0 and router2:eth0 shows “Link detected: yes,” and both routers can ping each other on the interconnect (193.10.11.1 <-> 193.10.11.2), ruling out a cabling issue.

Mechanism: with bgpd stopped on router1, no BGP adjacency forms across 193.10.11.0/24, so router1 lacks a route to 200.1.1.0/24 and router2 lacks a route to 195.11.14.0/24. The hosts then receive immediate ICMP net-unreachable replies from their default gateways.

Localization: router1, FRRouting/bgpd process (control-plane), interfaces are up.

Root cause: FRR (bgpd) on router1 has failed/stopped, preventing BGP route exchange.

Corrective fix: restart the routing service on router1 (systemctl restart frr, or start bgpd if managed separately). After restart, verify “show ip bgp summary” shows Established on both routers and that routes to 195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24 appear; pc1↔pc2 pings should then succeed.
=== END ===